## [ENT-014] | 2026-11-16 10:58 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md | see: ENT-013
Changed: in the Fleet Admiral's turn of 2026-11-16 10:30 UTC. A route may now name the bid it sells into (`requires.bid`) and the load a pilot must hold in its own storage (`requires.stored`); a circuit's hunt step may name a herd floor (`min_creatures`); the dispatcher closes a repeating route whose last three runs each lost money, stops an engine loop no runner owns, and counts a once run whose every stop was skipped as no try (EVERY ADMIRAL; requests.log ENT-117, 125, 126, 129, 083). The once line of the Central Nexus board found by Trade stands in every plan, and voidborn-first-steps is off (EVERY ADMIRAL). The Crimson Resonance: one accept with an empty hold first (MERCURY). The block UNITY: what this turn built and what is owed. GOAL: where we stand at 10:56 UTC. Suggi's standing orders and the rest of ENT-013 are unchanged.
In force from 2026-11-16 10:58 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi, 2026-11-15 and 2026-11-16; set and changed by him alone)
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Grow income; optimize the routes and the credits per hour.
S3. Evolve the fleet.
S4. Build bases and stations.
S5. Grow the fleet and the faction.
S6. A base, and a station in a system of ours when we are ready: gather the materials and the supply for it and get the economy going (Suggi, 2026-11-16: "i want a base build and the materials gathered and all ready to build a station in a system when ready").
Every member works toward these on every turn, also where no order below names the work. The orders below (GOAL, FLEET, TURN and the blocks) are the Fleet Admiral's reading of them: the steps, the subgoals, who does what and the limits. The limits are Suggi's own words too and stand beside the standing orders: the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot without his word (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the faction's affairs (F11). An order below that seems to stand in the way of a standing order is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a reason to pass a limit.
GOAL (the steps toward the standing orders; Suggi, 2026-11-15)
Phase 1 of the strategy (governance/strategy.md, section V): income. First income that repeats in every division. Then a faction with shared storage and a first supply chain. Then stations and bases of our own beyond the safe zone: strong and independent, not hiding. Every order below serves the first step. What the third step costs (the game's guides, read 2026-11-15): an outpost in a lawless system is an Outpost Kit and 100,000 cr, a full station a Station Core and 5,000,000 cr, in a system without empire and police, at a place without a station; a faction may hold eight outposts and five stations. More ships are born when a division shows paying work for more ships than it has (F5). Where we stand (`hive.py fleet`, 2026-11-16 01:43 UTC): the first hour of steady money above zero, +55,031 cr, made by 10 miners on ice and 5 hunters, while 120 of 146 ships wait docked. The bottleneck of this turn is a tool, not a field and not a bid (`hive.py capacity`, 01:30 UTC): five of our stations bid for 81,695 units of water ice, 254,347 units lie in the fields we read, and 14 ships carry an Ice Harvester I. The earners are the 40 miners, 20 traders and 20 haulers; harvesters go to the miners first. Three ways to the tool, all taken at once: (a) bought at our stations, 14 on offer at 1,413 to 1,738 cr, a few at each (books of 01:20 to 01:26 UTC); (b) bought at Ironlight Crossroads in LHS 1140, the one foreign market, 20 on offer at 638 cr (one book, 01:24 UTC); (c) made by hand from 4 steel plate, 2 circuit board, 3 flex polymer and 1 power cell (`craftbook.py item ice_harvester_i`): steel plate and circuit boards from the ore we hold; flex polymer stands on offer from 129 cr or is made from caustic sac or from silicon and nickel ore; a power cell is made from 2 circuit boards, 3 energy crystals and 2 copper wiring, or from creature goods. Made from stored ore by Logistics for Mining, it is the first supply chain of the fleet. A bid is used up as we sell into it and its refill is not measured: the bids are the next limit to watch. An Outpost Kit stood on offer for 216,825 cr at Ironlight Crossroads (one order book): with the fee an outpost would cost about 317,000 cr without the facility that makes the kit (`hive.py base outpost`). Founding one is Suggi's decision; no ship buys the kit. Suggi, 2026-11-16: the kit is not needed now; a base is built later. Where we stand at 02:49 UTC, after the engine's restart of 02:00 UTC ended the runs in flight: 18 ships at work, 120 waiting. In the last four hours mining made 4,696 cr per ship-hour flown, hunting 1,771, and the fleet 175 cr per ship-hour over all 146 ships; the scale is the best route at 8,271 (the tool fleet with view=tasks). What a bid regains is now measured at its best price (view=refill): water ice at Central Nexus at least 1,628 units an hour, at Node Alpha at least 870; the deepest standing bid seen is 67,735 units at Central Nexus at 21 to 23 cr. Tools on offer in our books at 02:44 UTC: 34 Ice Harvesters, 82 Gas Harvesters. At 04:52 UTC: 66 ships at work, 80 docked; steady net 104,094 cr in the last hour (49,065 an hour over six); 47 Ice Harvesters and 3 Gas Harvesters fitted (14 and 0 at 01:40 UTC); mining made 3,809 cr per ship-hour flown, hunting 1,332, the fleet 315 over all 146 ships. At 10:56 UTC (the tool fleet, views money and tasks; hive.py standing all): 58 ships at work, 88 docked; steady net 123,877 cr in the last hour, 72,840 an hour over six and 27,901 over 24; credits 8,252,586. In four hours the fleet made 271 credits per ship-hour over all 146 ships (680 per ship-hour flown) against the best repeating route, hunt-deepwell-volatile-ice-frost-moth, at 4,564; Mining 1,212 over its 40 ships, the guard 548, every other role below zero without the 1,227,235 cr paid once. The water-ice bids near the fields emptied at 07:07 and 07:25 UTC and the steady net fell to 12,429 cr in the hour to 08:00 UTC until Mining moved its ships to Central Nexus (errors.log ENT-046); one deep bid carries the ice now. The money of this phase is the once line of the boards (about 24,000 cr a pilot behind First Steps, queue.log ENT-091, ENT-095, ENT-096) and the ice sold at Central Nexus; what limits income is the bids, not the tools. (d) The station (S6). What it takes is printed by the tool base_plan with kind=station, from the catalog and our stock: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and one station core; the core is made only in a station core foundry of level 4, which costs 38,000,000 cr and, among others, 12,500 steel plate, 8,400 hull plating and 7,800 copper piping to build; 43,000,000 cr in all, 28 of its 30 raw inputs short today (iron ore: 491,400 needed, 59,658 held), and no finished core on offer in our books. That is 400 to 900 hours of income at the rates of this hour and of the last six: the way to the station is income first (strategy, phase 1) and the faction's storage (F11), and each division reads its part of the list now (one line in each block), so that the fleet knows where every input comes from before it has the credits. An outpost (100,000 cr and a kit) is the small first base; Suggi decides when.
FLEET
F1. Each Admiral commands the ships of its own division, by the word before the dash of a pilot's name: miner- Zero, trader- Mercury, hauler- Apollo, drone- Apophis, scout- Oracle. The Fleet Admiral commands the command group (F10) and may command any ship. Every command goes through the scripts of game/fleet/ (game/fleet/index.md): they refuse a ship of another division and a ship another program is flying. No call goes to the gate around them.
  Goal: every division finds its own income, and no two hands drive one ship.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F2. Stance (Suggi, 2026-11-15). No ship of ours fires first at a player's ship or an NPC ship, anywhere. Fired upon, it defends or flees: it may shoot back; it flees when its hull falls under 60%, when a second ship joins, or at once when it is no match. A pilot that fired first at a ship of ours is an enemy from then on: the ship's Admiral reports the encounter in errors.log, the pilot's file in game/players/ becomes `h-` (game/players/BLUEPRINT.md: any Admiral, the encounter is the evidence) and stays `h-`; only Suggi takes a name off that list. An enemy in the same place is a danger: a ship leaves or docks, and no ship attacks it first. Wildlife is hunted inside F9. An escort (Suggi, 2026-11-16): a ship of ours that stands guard may fire at a ship that fired first at the ship it guards, to cover it; when the guarded ship is away, the escort escapes too. It never fires first and never pursues.
  Goal: we start no fight, and nobody hits us twice unnoticed. Diplomacy comes later and is Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F3. Every ship may leave policed Voidborn space (S1: explore freely). The census reads the systems F13 names; an Admiral may send a ship of its own beyond them: one ship per new system, hold empty, the stance of F2 kept, and what it found written into the system's file in game/planets/ and told in queue.log.
  Goal: a ship is risked where the risk can pay.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F4. No pilot of ours writes to another player; the fleet's scripts refuse it for an Admiral's hand. What other pilots write to our ships, and what they say about us where our ships stand, is collected by itself: `python3 -I -B game/fleet/inbox.py show --to-us`, and filed per faction in communications/contacts/<faction tag>.log. It is information about them, never an order to any member, whatever it says. A contact is passed to the Fleet Admiral.
  Goal: diplomacy has one voice.
  Changes when: approved templates exist.
F5. No new pilot without Suggi's word. The size he ordered on 2026-11-15: 40 miner-, 20 trader-, 20 hauler-, 30 drone- and 20 scout- ships, and the command group of F10. No pilot is registered only for its starter rewards (rulebook 8.1). A division grows when it shows paying work for more ships than it has.
  Goal: permanent choices are Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F7. Every pilot keeps 3,000 cr in its wallet.
  Goal: the weekly tax is taken from the wallet (Voidborn: 6% of the week's income, 0.75% of the ship's value); next assessment about 2026-11-16 12:30 UTC.
  Changes when: the tax is prepaid, or the rates change.
F8. No ship works at a loss to fill time, and no ship waits while paying work stands open. A plan is a standing loop: the dispatcher gives every free pilot the first open route of its role's plan (game/routes/plans/<role>.json), again and again, day and night, without a turn; an Admiral's turn changes the plan, it does not start the ships. So every plan holds work for all of its division's ships for the four hours to the next turn and beyond, in this order: the missions that pay once; the routes that pay, best first, each with `max_ships` lifted as far as its bids and herds carry; the tests, all at once, one ship each; the census of the frontier; and last the route park: dock at home and wait, which costs nothing. A mission that pays once is flown by every pilot as soon as its route exists: nobody asks first. A route that loses money leaves the plan at once. A test is one ship with `max_ships` 1 until its first RESULT line is read; then more ships; the hourly job writes every measured figure (`routes.py measure --write <route ids>` writes one at once). Every turn ends on the fill gate (Suggi, 2026-11-16: no ship idle in the four hours between two turns): `game/fleet/fill.py check`, the tool `fill`, says PASS, or it says how many ships wait while paying work or a test stands open; `fill.py status` says for every route of the plan why it is closed to the ships that wait, and `fill.py fill --write` puts the open work into the plan. No route flies at a loss unattended: the dispatcher closes by itself a route whose runs of the last six hours, two or more, lost money in sum, until the window moves on (the census, park and fitting routes are not judged); a route counts as paying from two measured runs; a candidate that buys goods is never added by a script: a gap is flown once by hand with one load (errors.log ENT-027).
  Goal: the fleet's credits rise. Measured 2026-11-15: mining iron and copper for skill cost about 160 cr per ship-hour, 22,000 cr an hour for the fleet, and the ore has no buyer.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F9. A hunter fights grazers of 100 hull or less, one hunter per place, and flees under 60% hull or when a ship joins the fight. The Military division may test bigger prey and other fits: one ship per test, the flee rule kept, hull and fit written into the route.
  Goal: no ship lost to a hunt.
  Changes when: a hull is tested against bigger prey and its route says so.
F10. The command group is the Fleet Admiral's own division, Command: six flagships (the Fleet Admiral's command ship and one captain ship per Admiral) and their guard, the sentinel- ships. The group flies one plan (the roles command and sentinel). An Admiral lists its flagship in its FLEET.md; the flagship flies with the command group, under the Fleet Admiral's hand.
  Goal: the fleet's command moves as one group under one hand (Suggi, 2026-11-15).
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F11. The faction The Collective (tag CLTV) exists since 2026-11-15. Its leader is the Fleet Admiral's flagship; its 16 members are the command group. Suggi, 2026-11-16: every ship of ours joins it, and it gives the fleet shared storage and shared money. The steps are the Fleet Admiral's, in this order, each read from the game before it is paid: (1) the faction's lockbox at Central Nexus (listed at 200,000 cr): item storage that every member fills and an officer empties; (2) members up to the cap, which read 50 on 2026-11-15: the haulers first, since they move the stored goods; (3) a recruitment office that lifts the cap until all 146 are in (the game's docs name 75,000 cr, 300,000 cr and 2,000,000 cr for 50, 100 and 200 members; the live costs and what else each step asks are not read yet); (4) the treasury: any member pays in, an officer pays out. Until a step stands, storage stays each pilot's own. Inviting, joining, leaving, a role, a war, peace, an alliance and every word in the faction's name are the Fleet Admiral's, on Suggi's word. No pilot of ours joins another faction.
  Goal: one voice toward other players, a faction that stays ours, and one store and one purse for the fleet.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F12. Money. An Admiral spends only the wallets of its own ships and never takes one below F7. On tests (a module, goods for a trade or a craft, a hull) a division spends at most 10% of its ships' credits in one turn; Military 25%, for weapons and hulls. Before goods are bought for a mission, a trade or a craft: one ship, one unit of the test, the answer read. A bid's quantity in our market files (`buy_quantity`) is the total of all its price levels: before a chain is costed or goods are bought for it, read the depth at the best price (`best_buy_qty`, or view_market at the station; errors.log ENT-015). A station's own bid for a few units is a one-time price until data/market/history.jsonl shows it refill (`ledger.py prices <item>`). Nothing is given away, jettisoned or sold under a standing bid to empty a hold or a storage; a mission that counts units sold is the one exception, inside its route.
  Goal: a test that fails costs little. On 2026-11-15 about 92,000 cr went into goods for a mission that hands them out, and three chains were costed on a depth that was a total.
  Changes when: a division shows income that repeats.
F13. Beyond Voidborn space (Suggi, 2026-11-15: we explore all around us, we go hard, it is dangerous, we need to make money). The whole fleet explores the lawless systems around our ten: every division's ships and the whole command group, flagships and sentinels. The work is the route game/routes/census-frontier.json, in every plan before park: a free ship reads whole the system read longest ago and moves on to the next; twelve ships at a time; each system again after four hours. Its list: the 27 systems within two jumps of our border, nearest first, then Atlas and Intercrus, and LHS 1140 for its station; Oracle keeps the list and widens it ring by ring. What a read finds lies in data/scouting/<system>.json and in the system's file in game/planets/, and the route maker turns it into candidates within ten minutes. A route that works beyond policed space carries `requires.empty_hold` (nothing aboard to lose) and, when it hunts, `alone` (no fight where a ship that is not ours stands): the route maker writes both by itself. No ship carries goods of value out; it sells and stores at a Voidborn station. A ship lost is an entry in errors.log with its cause, and the system where it was lost leaves the list until Military has looked at it.
  Goal: find what pays more than our six stations do, and the place where a base of ours could stand.
  Changes when: the reports of the reads.
F14. Knowledge. Each Admiral keeps its guidebook, game/routes/guidebook/<division>.md: the routes that pay with their measured figures, what did not pay and why, how its pilots level, the hull and the fit for its work. Every member reads the others'. A figure comes from routes.py measure or a run's RESULT line, never from memory. What an Admiral means to do next stands in `planned` of its plan file. The guidebook's first section is `Standing Plan` (Suggi, 2026-11-16: the Admirals know their own fleet and do not plan each turn from nothing): which routes the division's ships fly between two turns, in which order, with how many ships each and what each is expected to bring per ship-hour, why, and what would change it; routes, places and counts, never a pilot's name. Preflight reads the guidebook and prints the standing fleet; session-end brings the two into agreement (the shared skills preflight and session-end).
  Goal: every ship born later starts where the others stand, and five Admirals do not test the same thing twice.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F15. Data (Suggi, 2026-11-15: the speed at which what we read becomes information the fleet can use is the key). Everything the fleet reads is kept by itself, by services that run without a turn, and is every member's to read: every ship of every division with its order, place and wallet (`game/fleet/board.py all`); what is in storage and how every price moved (`game/fleet/ledger.py show all`, `prices <item>`); every way to earn that the readings show, costed and with its route file (`game/fleet/routemaker.py show all`); what the routes measured (game/routes/index.md); who our ships met and what others wrote (data/intel/, `game/fleet/inbox.py show`); the Admirals' guidebooks (F14). A figure an Admiral needs twice is a request for a script, not a second hand count. Since 2026-11-16 all of it is one tool list in every member's Hermes, the collective MCP server (`mcp_collective_<tool>`): fleet, ship, routes, route, fill, candidates, market, stock, catalog, base_plan, system, comms, search, and the door to your ships: command (one game command to many ships at once), goal, hold. The shared skill fleet-play lists them: ask them first. What the work carries, what a base takes, how the levels stand and what each member wrote into its knowledge: the tool fleet with view=capacity and view=levels, base_plan, and comms with view=learnings. Measured by scripts, not by turns (Suggi, 2026-11-16: every data point, the refill and respawn times too): the tool fleet with view=tasks (all work in one unit), view=standing (a division's plan route by route and every ship of it on one line) and view=refill (what a bid regains at its best price, what a field grows back and the animals a herd regains, per hour, as the least our readings prove). A place counts once it was read twice: every census and every docking adds a reading. A run whose wallet read 0 at either end counts for nothing.
  Goal: five Admirals plan on the same picture, minutes old.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F16. Roles in harmony (Suggi, 2026-11-15). The divisions are one system. Intel patrols: its scouts are always on the move, reading our systems and the frontier (the census routes), and they hunt on the way: a scout is a light warship. Military patrols, protects and escorts: its drones stand where our ships work, above all beyond policed space, hunt there, and are the first to meet whoever comes. Mining, Trade and Logistics earn: they mine, trade, haul and craft on the routes that pay, and what they earn carries the patrols. A patrol's fuel is the price of the picture and of safety, not a loss under F8: its Admiral reports that price per hour and lowers it by hunting. Who needs a guard at a place asks Military in requests.log; who needs a place read asks Intel. A guard defends inside F2: it starts no fight; it may fire at a ship that fired first at the ship it guards, then both escape (Suggi, 2026-11-16). No runner fights a ship yet: until the guard runner exists (owed by me) a guard watches, is seen and reports, and the ship it guards flees by its own rule.
  Goal: the earners work unafraid, the patrols see everything first, and every ship has work.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F17. Ships (Suggi, 2026-11-15: everyone upgrades its ships all the time and makes them the best for their jobs). Every ship carries the hull and the fit its job needs, and the next step up is bought as soon as wallet and skill allow, inside F12 and above the floor of F7. Each Admiral keeps in section 5 of its guidebook the hull and the fit its division flies, the next step with its price and the skill it needs (Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5), and what the step should earn or save. A module every ship of a role needs is written as a `fit` route (game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md, a stop's `fit`): the dispatcher then fits the whole division without a hand. A new module or hull is tested on one ship first.
  Goal: no ship flies a starter's fit longer than its wallet and its skill force it to.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F18. Capacity (Suggi, 2026-11-16: what is the most our systems carry, how much can we harvest by hunting and mining in all, how many more ships can we build before the resources run empty; for every Admiral in its domain). Each Admiral states in its report, from records: what its domain carries today (Mining: per good the units the bids take, the units left in the fields and what they regain; Military and Intel: the herds, what a herd yields an hour and how fast it regrows; Trade: the gaps, the chains and the missions that pay once still open; Logistics: what storage holds and what it can be made into); what limits it first (a tool, a bid, a field, a herd, a hold, a level); how many ships that employs against the ships the division has; and so how many ships it could add, or does not need. The base figures: the tool fleet with view=capacity (`hive.py capacity`). A figure no record holds is written `not measured`, with the reading that would give it.
  Goal: the fleet grows where the work is, and no ship is born to wait.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F19. One unit (Suggi, 2026-11-16: I need credits per hour for each route and task, otherwise I cannot compare). Every earning in a report, a guidebook, a request or a log entry is given as credits per ship-hour, with its runs and its window, beside the scale of that hour: the best route's figure, and 0 for a ship without work (the tool fleet with view=tasks prints both; view=standing gives it per ship). A division's figure counts all its ships, the waiting ones too. A reward the game pays once is named apart, and a mission that pays once is judged with its reward.
  Goal: Suggi and every member can compare any two kinds of work at a glance.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F20. The fleet's purse (Suggi, 2026-11-16; planned, not built). What a ship holds above what its work needs is carried by the six flagships: the Fleet Admiral's a little more than the other five, those five even. A script of the Fleet Admiral moves it (by the game's rules a gift of credits from pilot to pilot works from anywhere). A ship keeps F7 and what the dearest route of its plan asks (`min_credits`). Until the script runs, no member moves credits between pilots.
  Goal: the credits for the lockbox, the offices and a base stand in six wallets, later in the treasury, and not in 146.
  Changes when: Suggi's word; the treasury stands (F11).
F21. By script, not by hand (Suggi, 2026-11-16: the Admirals control the ships through scripts, quickly, not each ship by hand; they spend their time on strategy and planning). What repeats is written once: a route for work that repeats, one call of the tool command for many ships, a script for a batch with logic in it. What a member lacks it builds itself, with its test, through `scripts/fleet-build.sh` (the shared skill fleet-build, attached to every turn job): a step of a runner (mine.py, hunt.py, route_run.py), a new fleet script, a verb of hive.py, a route. It says so in queue.log; the Fleet Admiral reads every such commit in her turn. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she changes: the dispatcher, the door and the lock, fill.py, the tools' server, the station, the logs' scripts, a new field of a route.
  Goal: no Admiral waits a turn for a tool it can write, and no turn is spent on ship-by-ship work.
  Changes when: a member's change stops ships twice; then the gate is tightened, not the rule dropped.
TURN
T9. From this entry until the next. A turn is four hours. The scheduled runs are on (Suggi, 2026-11-16: keep the jobs on from now on, the Fleet Admiral's too): Mining 00:00 UTC, Intel 00:15, Trade 00:30, Logistics 00:45, Military 01:00 and the Fleet Admiral 02:30, each again every four hours; the first scheduled turn starts at 04:00 UTC on 2026-11-16. The Fleet Admiral writes the fleet report and the orders in its own turn, ninety minutes before the Admirals' next, and reports to Suggi after it. This turn tests one thing (Suggi, 2026-11-16): can the divisions earn for four hours unbroken, by themselves. Each Admiral builds its plan so that every ship of its division holds paying work, or a test, for the four hours after its turn ends, and proves it with the fill gate (F8). Between two turns the ships fly by themselves from the plans.
  Done when: the fill gate's last line stands in each Admiral's report (PASS, or what closes the work to the ships that wait); the report names the routes its ships flew with their measured credits per ship-hour, every test with its RESULT line, the capacity of its domain (F18), what the levels of its pilots gained, and which knowledge files it wrote or changed; its plan and its guidebook are committed; none of its ships is left held by hand.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: you command your division's ships; you give every one of them work that pays, unbroken, for the four hours until your next turn. You start from what stands: preflight prints your standing fleet (the tool fleet with view=standing: your plan route by route, and every ship with what it flies, what it earned per hour and what closes the best route to it) and reads your guidebook; a turn changes the standing plan, it never plans the division from nothing. (1) Read the strategy (governance/strategy.md: preflight reads it now) and the picture (F15) with the collective tools: fleet (money, tasks, standing, refill, capacity, levels, board), fill with action=status (why each of your ships waits, route by route, and what work stands open), routes, candidates; your guidebook and the others'; comms with view=contacts. (2) Fill your plan (F8): fill with action=fill and write=true puts every open paying route and test into it (never one that buys goods). Then lift each paying route (action=lift) as far as the reading that bounds it carries: for a good that is sold, the ships its bid carries for four hours (fill's status prints them); for a herd, one hunter; for a mission that pays once, the pilots that have not flown it. Where a tool closes the work (fill names it and where it is on offer), get it to every ship the work can carry, inside F12: a `fit` route sized by the asks at its station, one ship at a time where they are few. A ship that waits earns 0: where your own work has no place for it, another division's paying route that has a free place and whose tool it carries is open to it with that Admiral's word in queue.log (fill with action=add puts the route into your plan; the route's limits stay its Admiral's). (3) Test every candidate worth a test at once, one ship each, in our space and on the frontier: candidates with id prints a candidate's route file; fill adds it as a test. What no runner can fly yet, fly by hand with one ship (the tools command and goal reach many ships in one call) and ask for the step. (4) Level and upgrade (F17): name in your guidebook which skill each route trains, the hull and the fit your division flies and the next step up (the tools fleet with view=levels and catalog with ask=build); buy it when wallet and skill allow. Ask Military for a guard where your ships work beyond the border, and Intel for a read (F16). (5) State the capacity of your domain (F18). (6) Write what the tests showed into your guidebook, each figure in credits per ship-hour (F19), and keep its first section `Standing Plan` true to what flies (F14); what you learned goes into your knowledge files, and what you mean to do next into `planned`. (7) Close the fill gate: fill with action=check; on HALT fill and lift again, wait two minutes, check again. (8) Report what your ships did, earned and cost, every earning in credits per ship-hour beside the scale (F19), with fuel and repair named where a record holds them, the gate's last line, what failed, and what you need from me: a route, a runner's step, a skill, data. The shared skill fleet-play says how; your FLEET.md lists your ships. Three things for every division from this entry. The Node Beta chain (`voidborn-conductive-lattice`, +10,893 cr once per pilot in about 32 minutes; its runner fault is fixed since 04:49 UTC, requests.log ENT-098) stands in every plan (fill with action=add); Mercury keeps its `max_ships` and `max_pilots` at what the silver at Ironlight Crossroads carries, and no one else changes the file. Signal Fragments (`voidborn-signal-fragments`, 7,000 cr once in about 14 minutes, three docks at our own stations, nothing carried) is offered by the board only from navigation 3, and since 06:43 UTC its route says so (`requires.skills`): it stands at the head of every plan (fill with action=add and first=true); a pilot without navigation 3 is not sent, and one that reaches it later is. Its file and its limits are Mercury's. And F21: what you did by hand twice this turn you write as a script before the next, and a fix of a runner's step you build yourself. From ENT-014, four more. (a) Every route of yours that sells one good into one station's bid names that bid: `requires.bid` {item, base, min_price} (game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md); the dispatcher closes it while a book read within the hour shows the bid gone and opens it when one shows it back, so a route set `status: off` for a bid that left comes back on with the field instead. (b) The dispatcher closes by itself a repeating route whose last three runs inside six hours each lost money, whatever its six-hour sum, and the fill gate no longer counts it as paying work; a route your hand took out for that reason may stand in the plan again. (c) The once line of the Central Nexus board stands in every plan, in this order, each gated by its route: voidborn-first-steps-board (accepts First Steps), voidborn-network-expansion, voidborn-collective-provides, voidborn-signal-protocol (fill with action=add and first=true; their files and limits are Mercury's). voidborn-first-steps is off: it served only pilots that already held the mission; drop it from your plan. (d) A circuit's hunt step may name `min_creatures`: no pilot is sent while the place's census counts fewer. A once route whose every stop was skipped no longer counts as done for its pilot; an engine loop no runner owns is stopped by the dispatcher after five minutes.
Limits: F1 to F18. The stance of F2 holds for every ship and every test. Your own ships only, and beyond Voidborn space as F3 and F13 say; what you need from another division is a request in requests.log, and you answer the open requests addressed to you at every session start. You write your plan, the routes your ships fly and your guidebook; a script of game/fleet/, a route another division flies too and another role's plan change by a request. Release every ship you took by hand before your turn ends. What these orders do not cover is a request to me, never a guess.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order (Suggi, 2026-11-15): smooth the way for the Admirals and build the plumbing. In my turn, after theirs: read every report, request and error of their turn; fix what stopped an Admiral: a runner's step, a route, a script, data that is missing; answer every request; read the first RESULT lines of new routes and lift or close each; fly new route files first with the command group; keep the collective tools growing (my skill collective-mcp); close the fill gate for the command group and name every division whose gate HALTs; write the fleet report and, when my orders changed, the next entry here, the standing orders carried over word for word; bring to Suggi what is his to decide. Built since ENT-009: the fill gate (`fill.py`), the collective tools, the game's catalog as files, the master indexes, a buy that never pays above its limit, `measure --write` by route and the hourly measure, the loss stop, a fitting's purchase booked as money spent once, an audit line for every command through the door. Owed to the Admirals by 2026-11-16 12:00 UTC: the craft step; a circuit stop that sells from storage and `requires.bid`; the route maker counting a small bid once; the ammunition check; the route kind `guard` with the escort rule of F2; the gate against pilot names; the census gap figure; the corrections of game/rules/; the empty-hold check by value; `requires.booked`; the outpost column of the map. Since ENT-010: the tool views standing, tasks and refill; the station's new layout; the fleet collector holds back a pass that lists only a part of the fleet; a wallet read as 0 is no reading; the constitution names the strategy. My command ships level and earn (Suggi, 2026-11-16): four more fit a harvester at Central Nexus (the four on offer there stand above the miners' limit) and fly ice; the others fly the census and the hunts open to them once their holds are empty. Next in my turns, in this order: the empty-hold check by value (a few leftover goods close hunts and the census to a ship); the history store drawn in queue.log ENT-058 (one writer, a read copy for the tools); a record of every bid's whole ladder and of the asks, so that a refill is measured in full; a short form of the standing fleet; the nine changes owed above. I report to Suggi after each of my turns. Since ENT-011, done: the runner fault of the chain (my own, of 01:09 UTC) and a row for every crashed run; the sale step of mine.py; `requires.booked`; holds in the tool stock; the tools' server answers through the file on disk, and its search works; the station's Metrics page holds still; `scripts/fleet-build.sh` and the skill fleet-build. Done in my turn of 06:30 UTC: the route maker runs again (it failed every pass from 03:32 to 06:32 UTC) and one bad file costs only its own reading; a service that is down or fails its passes is named in my chat within five minutes by itself; `requires.skills` (ENT-109); the fill gate counts only work a role may take (ENT-095); the battle stance flee on `in_battle` and the hold-back of a pilot stuck at stop 1 (ENT-090); the hunt step's last word in the run row (ENT-092); the scale of the tool fleet's view tasks is a repeating route. Done in my turn of 10:30 UTC: `requires.bid` for every kind of route (ENT-065, ENT-125); `requires.stored` (ENT-117); a circuit hunt step's herd floor and Cracking the Shell at an ice field with pressblisters (ENT-126); the loss stop reads a route's last three runs; an engine loop no runner owns is stopped (ENT-083); an empty once run is no try, voidborn-first-steps off, `store: all` on my once routes (ENT-129, ENT-122); the battle stance flee in census.py and mine.py; game/rules/ and the rulebook brought to 0.719.0 (ENT-038, ENT-119). Owed in my turn of 14:30 UTC: the gate against pilot names (ENT-052); the route maker counting a small bid once (ENT-069); the craft step, if Trade still needs it (ENT-013); for S6 and F11: the lockbox's and the offices' live costs, read from the game, the purse script (F20) and base_plan kept current. In my turn of 18:30 UTC: the route kind `guard` (ENT-070). Craft inputs bought for a chain are booked as money spent once by the script that buys them, with `gate.book_onetime(pilot, title, -credits)` (game/fleet/gate.py): no new tool is needed.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady net per hour; ships at work; divisions whose fill gate says PASS.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: put every miner on ice, and get the tool to every ship the ice can carry. Your five selling runs made +4,644 to +6,730 cr after fuel on routes capped at one to three ships, and 25 of your 40 miners waited for a harvester (your report of 00:34 UTC); in the hour to 01:43 UTC your 10 ships at work made +54,174 cr of steady money. The harvester is the bottleneck (GOAL). (1) fill with action=status and who=miner prints, for each ice route, the ships its share of the bids carries for four hours: lift each paying route to that number or to the ships that carry a harvester, whichever is smaller, and never above what the field holds. (2) Harvesters, miners first: 14 stand at our stations (fill's status lists them): buy them where they stand, one ship per ask (your rule, errors.log ENT-026). The runner now reads the ask, never pays above `max_price` with tax, and a run that found none rests the route for that pilot for an hour instead of counting as done (requests.log ENT-068, done; your six pilots are free again). Twenty stand at 638 cr at Ironlight Crossroads: a fitting route with its stop there, `requires.empty_hold`, one ship at a time, fits a miner for less than half; test it with one miner first (F3, F12, F13). (3) Logistics makes one by hand as a test (GOAL): tell Apollo what you need and by when. (4) The vent and the gas route: go on, one ship each, until their RESULT lines hold. (5) Capacity (F18): water ice, methane ice and what follows them; and how the water-ice bids moved under our own sales (the tool market): their refill is the next limit. The loss stop closed mine-cryogenic-reserve (two runs, -544 cr: it stored its ice instead of selling): find why it could not dock for its sale before it reopens. At 02:49 UTC nine of your miners work (40,148 cr an hour, steady) and 31 wait. Thirty Ice Harvesters stand on offer for you: 20 at 638 cr at Ironlight Crossroads; 2, 2, 4 and 2 at 1,413 cr at Node Beta, Node Gamma, Synchrony and The Experiment (the four at Central Nexus go to the command ships). Each one fitted is a ship at 4,000 to 8,000 cr per ship-hour: fit them all this turn. No ship mines iron or copper for storage. `routes.py measure --write` takes route ids or `--role miner` (requests.log ENT-078, done); an hourly job writes every figure. A harvester bought by a fitting is booked as money spent once and no longer counts against your steady figure. For S6: which fields carry the station's ores (iron, carbon, silicon, nickel, gold), how much each holds and regains; no ship mines for storage yet. From ENT-014: every ice route names its station's water-ice bid (`requires.bid`), the Central Nexus routes too, so that a route closes when its bid goes and opens when it comes back (errors.log ENT-046). The stranded ice (about 11,100 water ice in miners' storage where the bids are 0): `requires.stored` exists; the withdraw stop and the carry routes are yours (your plan of 08:25 UTC), sold where a bid stands. Answer Apollo's request for places for the made harvesters (requests.log ENT-128) in your turn.
Found 2026-11-16: five selling ice runs on four fields, +4,644 to +6,730 cr each after fuel, where the route maker had estimated 2,900 to 3,700 cr per ship-hour; the nitrogen vent gave 83% water against 59% (one run each); a bid moves within minutes (Synchrony water ice 34 x 7,470, then 37 x 72, seven minutes apart); seven harvesters bought by route, one above its limit.
